[FINAL-TEXT] The picture is complete. router1:eth0 = `193.10.11.1/24`, router2:eth0 = `193.10.11.2/24`. router2's neighbor config (`neighbor 193.10.11.1 remote-as 1`) is **correct** — it points at the right IP and right AS. The sole problem is that **router1 has no `neighbor` statement at all**.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The FRR BGP daemon (`bgpd`) on router1 is missing its eBGP peer declaration entirely. The `router bgp 1` block contains only the `network 195.11.14.0/24` advertisement — there is no `neighbor` line.

**Mechanism:** BGP requires both endpoints to have the peer configured. router2 correctly declares `neighbor 193.10.11.1 remote-as 1` and keeps opening TCP port 179 toward router1, which is why router2 shows the session in `Active` state (perpetual retry). Because router1 has no `neighbor 193.10.11.2 remote-as 2` entry, it never initiates a session itself and does not accept or process inbound BGP connections from an unconfigured peer. The session therefore never reaches `Established`, so no prefixes are exchanged in either direction. Without the BGP-learned routes, router1's RIB has no path to 200.1.1.0/24 and router2's RIB has no path to 195.11.14.0/24. Both routers return ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable" to their locally-attached hosts for any cross-AS traffic.

**Localization:** router1, FRR `router bgp 1` configuration block.

**Root cause:** router1 is missing the eBGP neighbor statement `neighbor 193.10.11.2 remote-as 2`, causing the inter-AS BGP session to never establish.

**Fix:** Under `router bgp 1` on router1, add:
```
neighbor 193.10.11.2 remote-as 2
```
Once the session comes up, both routers will exchange their `network` prefixes and full pc1↔pc2 reachability will be restored.
=== END ===